A||||20||1slow60||Hey computer guys PROM question||||1||
Z||000000||1slow60||12-26-2001||08:11 PM||||  I have an automatic E PROM in my computer and am running a 5 speed.  Can and will this work correctly?  I am experiencing problems going into closed loop.  Do I need to do anything with the wiring harness were it hooked into the auto tranny?  The ECM is a 7165 with a hypertech chip for a 350 TPI.  Thanks for any help guys.<p>[This message has been edited by 1slow60 (edited 12-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||rockcrawl||12-26-2001||09:50 PM||||I don't think any of the trans connections on the 7165 will prevent closed loop. Do you have a scanner?||||reg||1
Z||000002||1slow60||12-26-2001||10:11 PM||||  Yes i have a scanner.  It says that od is disengaged, torque converter is locked, and it is in third gear.  But all this doesnt really mean anything to me.  I have heard of people running auto proms with stick shifts but I dont know if they had to make any adjustments to the wiring to make it work properly.  Anyone know for sure?||||reg||1
Z||000003||00lE||12-26-2001||10:25 PM||||Unless you are actually running a 350 V8, and assuming this is a Fiero V6 you are talking about (a little sparse on details eh?) I would suggest obtaining the correct 5-spd Fiero ECM and installing it.  Its not like they are a real expensive item, they can be found for 10-20 bucks on eBay all the time.<p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 12-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||12-26-2001||10:32 PM||||Closed loop requires a certain CTS temp and O2 transitions. Hook the scanner up and watch the O2. If it's not swinging, it won't go closed loop or will drop out.<P>I think the temp is 116F, but I'd have to check.<P>The auto code will work, but there are benefits to the manual trans code.<P>What year is the mem-cal for? They did make some changes in 89.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||1slow60||12-26-2001||10:55 PM||||  Well I cant lie.  Its in a 86 pontiac firebird.  The motor, ECM, and wiring harness are all out of an 88 camaro.  I could find fiero parts a lot easier.  Only thing the bone yard has for f bodies around here is v6 models.  I am getting the engine temp at the cts senso.  As high as 230 degrees.  I am getting a crosscount as high as 211,  But the O2 mv stays right at 444 and only fluctuates about 10 mv either way.  This is with a new O2 sensor with 50 miles on it.  Gm rep said to replace it again with a GM o2 sensor.  I am begining to wonder if the PROM or chip is bad.  What you guys think?  The 89 code change was deletion of the cold start injector.  Then in 90 the went away from MAF systems.<p>[This message has been edited by 1slow60 (edited 12-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||1slow60||12-26-2001||11:00 PM||||the prom and memcal are in one chip.  think this started in 88.  The chip is a hypertech unit for an 88 350 camaro or GTA.||||reg||1
Z||000007||00lE||12-26-2001||11:54 PM||||Seems like it would set a few codes if what you are saying is correct, otherwise it should go closed loop in idle as soon as the min temp for hot C/L is met.  With the observations you describe of the O2 sensor behavior, I would say the sensor is bad.<BR>Not many manual bins for the 88 Camaro/Firebird TPI running a 7165, the 89 Vette APYP would be something to look into.  Auto trans code with a stick is just not really desirable, too many issues to deal with, even if you have a prom burner.||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||12-26-2001||11:54 PM||||It might be a new O2, but it's not connected. Check the wiring and O2 ground from the ECM to the chassis. It's possible you have it too far downstream to heat, but a nice 3 minute rev at 3000 will certainly warm it up and it should start switching.<P>The 440mV you are getting is the bias to the sensor. That comes out of the ECM. It's possible the ECM is toasted, but I seriously doubt it. Seriously.<P>The mem-cals (eproms) rarely go bad. Since you can scan it, the eprom is ok.<P>CTS for CL is 104F so you are clearly there.<P>The 7165 was in use from 86-89. The code is essentially the same. The deletion in the CSI in 89 was the big one.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||1slow60||12-27-2001||12:06 AM||||  The O2 sensor is a 1 wire model.  I know that at the bottom of the ECM lead where the O2 Sensor pigtail connects is getting .3v with the ignition on and engine off.  The sensor should be self grounded to the manifold-manifold to the block-block to the chasy.  Am I missing something on this thought?  O yea the last thing that i tried to iliminate any wiring issue was run a jumper wire straight from the ECM plug to the O2 sensor.  Same scanner readings so I dont think that the wire has an impedience problem.  Do you guys see anything that i missed here in trouble shooting this code slash circuit?  thanks guys  O yea I forgot to verify this but you are saying that the auto trans programing will work fine just not be optimal till I can afford to buy a new PROM?<p>[This message has been edited by 1slow60 (edited 12-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||00lE||12-27-2001||12:22 AM||||If it fails to enter closed loop after the hot closed loop temp has been achieved, a trouble code should set.  Depending on the code, it is likely that the ECM will never again enter closed loop until the code has been cleared, even if you replaced the O2 sensor.  Try clearing the codes by disconnecting the battery for a bit and see how it behaves.  It is not that rare to get a brand new O2 sensor that is defective, even more likely if you are buying something other than OEM GM sensors.<BR>Most of the issues with running auto trans code with a stick are idle related, and will be most pronounced when coming to a stop. (eg. Deceleration enleanment, etc.)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 12-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||1slow60||12-27-2001||12:27 AM||||  To clear the codes i pull the fuse out of the ECM power wire next to the battery.  I will leave it out for 30 seconds if i am in a hurry.  Usually I smoke cigarette while i am waiting so that is at least 5 minutes.  This should suffice instead of pulling the battery cable correct?  I guess that I will have to call the chevy dealer tommorrow and price an 02 sensor.  thanks   As an after thought I checked alot of the Grounds from the ECM with an ohm meter to the cigarette lighter ground all seemed sound.  These were terminals a-12, d-1, d-3, d-6 (o2 sensor ground), d-10, aldl terminal a, Maf sensor terminal a, esc module d, along with the fuel pump and MAF power relay grounds.<p>[This message has been edited by 1slow60 (edited 12-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||12-27-2001||12:28 AM||||The O2 circuit is a differential input to the ECM. One side is grounded via a wire to the engine block. If that ground is bad, the housing ground to the manifold is irrelevant.<P>Scan it and connect a 1.5V battery to the O2 input. See if it drives it to 1.11V. That will tell you if the O2 input is working.<P>The ECM should go closed loop again if the hot closed loop condition is gone even if the code is stored.<P>00LE are you saying closed loop is disabled if the timer is active?<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||00lE||12-27-2001||12:33 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B><BR>The ECM should go closed loop again if the hot closed loop condition is gone even if the code is stored.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That would really depend on the code that was set, a lean exhaust code for example will prevent closed loop until it is cleared.<P>There are two power sources for the ECM, one switched and one constant(memory).  Its best to just pull the battery cable if in doubt.  Your scanner should easily be able to tell if there are trouble codes still in memory.<p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 12-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||terryk||12-27-2001||12:35 AM||||I talking TPI V8 code for the 7165.<P>He's not even getting the min and max for O2 ready (.2V and .7V). I just don't think the O2 is talking.<P>I'll be curious to see what he finds.||||reg||1
Z||000015||00lE||12-27-2001||12:43 AM||||If the scanner is indicating cross counts as he observed, then I would say that is a good indication of at least some O2 sensor operation.  Its pretty hard to see the actual O2 sensor voltage deviations on some scanners...||||reg||1
Z||000016||terryk||12-27-2001||12:48 AM||||"I am getting a crosscount as high as 211, But the O2 mv stays right at 444 and only fluctuates about 10 mv either way."<P>It's just hanging at the bias voltage. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||1slow60||12-27-2001||01:34 AM||||  I definatly aint an exprt with these computer rides but I think that the computer is just going on the bias that was mentioned.  I dont think that it is actually reading the sensor.  But I dont think that it the wiring.  This grond that you are mentioning,  would it be the one from the block to the firewall?  My crosscounts started linear then went up from * to 211.  I will take the scanner to work with me tommorrow and get a reading on the trip home.  I will clear the codes via the battery tterminals first though.  Let ya know what i find.  The scanners memory will be in frames that are every 1.4 seconds for 29 frames.  The scanner is an Actron scan tool if that helps yall.  Thanks for all the help guys.  This board has definatly been the most helpfully as of yet.  At this rate i will be doing one of those V8 fierro deals myself and I am a truck guy!!<p>[This message has been edited by 1slow60 (edited 12-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||12-27-2001||11:52 AM||||O2 grounds on single wire O2's are usually tan coming from the ECM and go to the block (water inlet or bell housing bolt). I don't have my 7165 diagrams handy.<P>When you start it cold, does the O2 start off at 450mV and then drop to &lt; 200mV as it warms and then climb to above 700mV or does it just sit at 450mV all of the time during warm up?<P>Did you try the 3 minute 3000 RPM rev to see if it switches? If you cruise it, it dang well better switch. The temp for CL enable is 102F in the code I just checked. Pretty low.||||reg||1
Z||000019||1slow60||12-27-2001||05:12 PM||||  Tried the rev the motor to no avail.  I checked the voltage today while driving and it hovers right at the 440-450 mark.  I think that it starts out there cold but I will have to check that.  Here in a little bit I am going to try a suggestion of giving the motor a shot of starting fluid to alter the air fuel ratio and see if it changes the sensor output.  But after pulling a plug loose yesterday and the O2 reading didnt move I think that maybe the sensor is bad.  Gonna reinstall the old one and see if i see a difference.  Also I am going to meter the O2 sensor with a multi meter just to insure that the ECM side aint screwing with the O2 readings going to the scanner.  I will let ya know.  Thanks for the help.||||reg||1
Z||000020||1slow60||12-28-2001||11:15 PM||||  The new O2 sensor didnt respond as good as the old one so I put the original back in it for the moment.  On the way to the shop the fuel pump died today so it is getting replaced tommorrow with a new AC Delco unit.  I wonder if i was loosing fuel preasure as the motors fuel reqirments increased before the pumps death?  This would create the lean conditions after the motor reved past idle.  I am not looking forward to pulling the rear axle to get to the gas tank but I will let ya know how she runs with the new pump installed.  Hopefully it does the trick.||||reg||1
